WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.379
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:08.280
Allison Butler, who's a contributor to the
Media and me, a guide to Critical Media Literacy

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.880
for Young People, and also the director of
the Media Literacy Certificate Program at

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.510
my undergraduate alma mater, the University
of Massachusetts in Amherst.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.119
Allison, it's really great having you on.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:20.150
I appreciate it.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.250
Speaker 3: Thanks so much.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.470
I'm really excited to be here.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.359
Speaker 1: So, I mean, I guess let's just
start with something.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:26.359
It's a simple question.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:30.310
The answer may not be so simple, but it's
something I talk about a lot.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:37.700
Media literacy, I think, should start being
taught at the latest in seventh grade to everybody.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.320
Probably fifth grade is even better.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.710
It's pretty rare, particularly, that public
schools have media literacy programs.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:49.660
Is it as simple as it's just not a priority
to those creating the curricula, or is there

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.290
more to why it's not commonly taught?

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:58.230
Speaker 3: I think there's more commonly taught
and I would raise your fifth, 2/7 grade to

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.850
say we need to start in early childhood education.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:06.150
Our children are exposed to the media within
immediately.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.580
And certainly with social media we can learn
a lot about them before they're born.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:14.570
Their identities are out there really early
on, I think.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:20.070
Yes, it is something that is really can be
understood to be difficult to add to the curriculum.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:25.570
And part of the reason why it's difficult
is we don't have formal training, formal education

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.340
for people who want to teach media literacy.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.020
So oftentimes teachers have to kind of figure
it out on their own and goodness knows they

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.890
have enough to do so.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:39.180
To ask them to add more is arduous.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.329
Part of our work.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:44.060
Part of my work is to say we need teacher
education and media literacy.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.340
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's interesting because
I remember that there were these kind of one

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:54.270
off classes in middle and high school that
were interesting.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:00.300
I remember one called like Transportation,
Power and Energy, which was really interesting.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.580
It didn't really fit anywhere.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:08.150
And the teacher who taught it was not certainly
an expert in that field and sort of had to

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.270
figure it out as as he went.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.519
And so it does seem that part of the problem,
as you're pointing out, is it's not common

00:02:14.519 --> 00:02:19.200
that the teacher base is necessarily equipped
to teach media literacy.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.660
Speaker 3: Correct.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.950
I talk with teachers all the time who say
that they're really interested in bringing

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:32.959
in the study of the media to their classrooms,
particularly because they see how glued to

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:35.430
their devices their students are.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.730
But then they're at a loss for how to teach
it formally.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:43.840
I think also sometimes, like you mentioned,
these kind of one off classes, they can often

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.370
be seen as like what you get to do when you're
done with what you're supposed to be.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.069
Speaker 1: Yes, yes, yes, absolutely.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:52.489
Speaker 3: Which is sure.

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:54.819
That's that's I mean, there's value in that,
right?

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:55.990
There's absolute value in that.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:00.000
I wouldn't say that that was that that was
that that's a bad idea.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.849
But I do think it's bad if it's the only way
that we're teaching it because our media are

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:07.280
so infused in our lives.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:13.980
Why would we then think about educating them
as like an add on or as something to do when

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.950
you're done the important stuff.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.360
Speaker 1: By the time people get to you.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:26.260
So at the college level, what do you find
are the most common blindspots in being able

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:32.620
to look at media and think about it critically
in terms of, you know, we often hear really

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:40.050
young kids can't tell the difference between
advertising and content, for example, or people

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.540
can't necessarily distinguish between whether
they're watching news or opinion programing

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.401
is a lot more common when for older people,
what are the most common blind spots by the

00:03:48.401 --> 00:03:50.510
time people are college age and get to you.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:55.810
Speaker 3: I mean, I'd say there's probably
two really big ones that can then branch out

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.170
into so many more.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.879
One, for the most part, by the time they get
to me, they're pretty good at understanding

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:05.120
the content, but they don't know where that
content comes from.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.120
They don't know the power behind the content,
and that's part of a lot of the work that

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.069
I do and that my colleagues and I do in in
the media.

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:18.340
NI is say it's not just about what's on the
screen or just what we're hearing, the actual

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.449
powers behind the scenes.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:24.590
We have to talk about ownership, production
and distribution, and I'd say connected to

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:25.590
that.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:31.190
But the other big area is, again, by the time
students come to my classroom as college students,

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:35.250
freshmen through seniors, they're really invested
in media.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.729
They're involved in a lot of media.

00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:42.520
They have multiple social media accounts,
but have no clue where their information is

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:43.860
going.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:51.120
Social media is a huge tool of surveillance,
massive amounts of data gathering.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.340
Probably safe to say that most, if not all
of us zip through those terms of service,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.900
zip through those privacy documents.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:05.150
Don't read the the truly, truly fine, fine
print and all the paragraphs of legalese,

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:06.410
we sign off on them.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:13.060
It's the only way that we have access to these
platforms, and yet we have no idea where our

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:16.260
information is going, what's happening to
it.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:22.389
The the one clue that we get is often as soon
as we search for something quite quickly after

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:25.770
that, an advertisement for that very thing
will pop up.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.120
We gave away the data that let that happen.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.729
Do you really dangerous?

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:37.100
Speaker 1: Do you find that over the last
ten years, the usage of social media among

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.450
18 to 22 year olds has changed in any appreciable
way.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.060
I mean, one of the things I've read is that
on average, people's use of Facebook is going

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:51.360
in the direction of fewer friends and tending
towards people you really know rather than

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.990
a larger circle of friends.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.449
Like that's a change in terms of average Facebook
use.

00:05:55.449 --> 00:06:00.390
But do you see that the 18 to 22 year olds
are thinking of their social media presences

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.230
differently than maybe a decade ago?

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.190
Speaker 3: Oh, absolutely.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.350
I mean, I think maybe some of what's going
on with young people in Facebook is that they

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:13.560
I mean, you know, once people my age got onto
Facebook, it became a lot less interesting

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:14.750
for young people.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.350
And, you know, now that grandma is there.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:18.350
Right?

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.350
Right.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.350
Makes it less fun, I guess.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.860
So I understand from my students that they
use something like Facebook almost more for

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.550
like school connections or school projects
as a way of connecting with people.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:33.790
But I think that in terms of their own personal
use of social media, it's a source of anxiety

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:39.410
that it's meant, at least at some points,
for them to be fun and as its name for it

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:40.580
to be social.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.020
But it can be remarkably antisocial.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.389
You're often pretty isolated with it, and
certainly some of those comparisons against

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:53.400
the beautiful airbrushed images are incredibly
difficult.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.870
But they're also entering a world where they're
expected to know how to use this professionally.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.810
And so that anxiety is not just about self,
but it's also about their preparation for

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:07.090
their future and what's sort of what's to
come next.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:12.090
And I think in some ways, like, like all of
us deal with certain areas of our life is

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:15.620
that young people can kind of become their
own worst enemies.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:20.050
They will be upset or concerned about once
it's introduced to them about some of these

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.100
surveillance technologies.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.250
But one of the biggest things that's happening
these days is that young people use the Find

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:31.639
My app, where they are constantly tracking
where their friends are.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:37.690
And what I hear from my students is they use
it so that when they don't text a friend back,

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.580
their friend knows it's because they're in
a class where they're not supposed to have

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.020
their phones on.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:48.020
So as much as they might get frustrated by
being surveilled, they also are participating

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.020
in that.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:55.240
Speaker 1: Speaking of phones in class, I
last taught as an adjunct at Boston College

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:00.310
in 2017 and it was a once a week class.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.909
So it was just like, Listen, we're here once
a week.

00:08:02.909 --> 00:08:05.530
I don't want to see any phones, period.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:07.340
Just absolutely no phones.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:08.630
They're on laptops.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:10.349
I don't know what they're doing on the laptops.

00:08:10.349 --> 00:08:12.800
I don't really want to get involved in that.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.710
But it was just like there should never be
a phone that's out.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.500
How much of an active distraction is whether
they're on social media or not, the phones

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.560
in the classroom right now?

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:23.560
What's your sense of that?

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.010
Speaker 3: Oh, I think there are huge distraction.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:27.889
It's so hard for us not to look at our phones.

00:08:27.889 --> 00:08:32.669
I actually have a policy in my class that
unless there is a documented accommodation,

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:38.339
need no cell phones, no computers and stop
looking at your smartwatches that we're here

00:08:38.339 --> 00:08:39.870
to discuss ideas.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.670
There's no tests in my class.

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.550
There's no final exam.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:49.149
This is about engaging with each other and
engaging with with our discussions.

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:51.970
And most of them can't really put their phones
away.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.019
They sort of turn them over on their desk.

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:57.770
Yeah, I will watch them get up and leave the
room for a few moments.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.779
And I'm assuming they're checking their phones,
but they don't want to get caught checking

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:02.779
it in class.

00:09:02.779 --> 00:09:05.780
And at the it there's there's stress about
that.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:07.930
And some of them do get frustrated with me.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:12.860
But at the end of the semester, the consistent
commentary I get is this is the one class

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:16.950
I actually paid attention to because I couldn't
be distracted.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.270
Speaker 1: That's interesting.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:22.120
That's interesting to to get kind of back
to the media literacy part.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:30.550
A couple other things, too, to talk about
when it comes to how to identify a trustworthy

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:31.940
source.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:36.620
Anybody who's been in this field any length
of time, we know about all of the things that

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.600
are often said.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.490
And it they tend to involve research.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.990
They tend to have you know, it's not oh, hey,
here's an article I just found.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:50.010
I'm very in 30 seconds going to be able to
do all of the things that on paper we often

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:53.160
say, here's how you identify whether a source
is trustworthy.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:59.120
What's the lowest hanging fruit that any news
consumer can employ to try to suss out whether

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.480
an article or a video clip or whatever the
case may be is coming from a source that can

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.480
be trusted.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.020
Speaker 3: I think the first thing to do is,
is an author mentioned because if an author

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.589
isn't mentioned, who knows how that got put
together?

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:13.220
It could be PR.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:14.640
Do you know the website?

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:15.640
Right.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.920
Does it and look closely at the website because
websites are easily manipulated.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:27.890
So you could have like, you know, New York
Times, dash com dot Gmail or, you know, and

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:33.110
then you're like, wait, I saw The New York
Times, but then it's something totally different,

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:34.110
right?

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:37.959
So truly look at the ending of the website,
almost eve of the URL almost before looking

00:10:37.959 --> 00:10:39.290
at the beginning.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:42.410
Does it look like a real Web site?

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.329
Can you get back to that article without to
I mean, look at everything is going to have

00:10:46.329 --> 00:10:47.329
pop up ads.

00:10:47.329 --> 00:10:52.490
But if it's the ones that are just like overtaking
your screen, can you learn more about the

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:53.490
author?

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.529
You don't have to like what they say, but
can you actually learn more about this person

00:10:57.529 --> 00:10:58.529
and is not?

00:10:58.529 --> 00:11:03.350
I would be suspicious about whether or not
that person is either actually a person or,

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.860
you know, potentially a bot or so on and so
forth.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:11.709
Or if it's somebody who is kind of hiding
behind some other sort of idea or some other

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:13.040
sort of message.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.149
So those would be the absolute first things.

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:20.410
I would also say be really cautious about
maybe the next level would be really cautious

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.920
about what you click on on social media.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.579
Social media platforms are not news platforms.

00:11:26.579 --> 00:11:29.420
We can share news on them.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.370
But they are not news platforms.

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:36.250
So if we you know, we talk to young people
who say like, oh, I get most of my news from

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:37.250
Twitter.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:38.250
Well, no, you don't.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.500
You get information on Twitter that somebody
might have shared.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.500
Right.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.500
That might be news.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:44.500
Speaker 1: You're finding links on Twitter.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:45.500
Exactly.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:46.500
Yeah.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.500
Speaker 3: You're finding links on Twitter.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:52.300
Speaker 1: Another thing that seems critical
that I tell people is to make an attempt to

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:56.490
get to the primary source if there actually
is one, because there's so, you know, there

00:11:56.490 --> 00:12:00.470
will be situations where not to pick on any
of these particular news outlets, but you'll

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:05.450
see a raw story article with a headline, and
it will quote Newsweek.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.829
It's actually a Newsweek article that they
are summarizing and editorializing.

00:12:09.829 --> 00:12:13.810
You go to the Newsweek article and Newsweek
actually says, as reported by The Washington

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:14.810
Post.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:20.399
And so you drilling down to where did this
actually come from seems like a fundamental

00:12:20.399 --> 00:12:24.930
first step in evaluating it, because really
all Ross story might be doing is rewriting

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:25.930
an article.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.570
You can't really ascertain the truth of the
matter.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:33.630
You can ascertain whether it's honestly rewritten,
but not the the underlying reporting.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:35.829
And so finding the primary source seems really
important.

00:12:35.829 --> 00:12:40.139
A lot of people may not realize the difference
between a rewritten blog style article and

00:12:40.139 --> 00:12:41.139
the reporting.

00:12:41.139 --> 00:12:42.790
Speaker 3: Yep.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:43.790
Absolutely.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:44.790
And that's true.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.760
I mean, I think one thing that you're absolutely
right is who is being sourced and our sources

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:54.470
being named are those the primary sources
is, you know, if something says something

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:58.019
to the effect of like research shows, well,
where is that research?

00:12:58.019 --> 00:13:03.310
I should be able I might not be able to distill
the research myself that quickly, but I should

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.550
be able to access the research.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:12.330
I also think that, especially in the previous
presidential administration, the idea of unnamed

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:13.330
sources.

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:14.330
Right.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:20.459
What does it mean for a source to go unnamed
for some for some stories that is really legitimate.

00:13:20.459 --> 00:13:21.839
There are dangerous issues.

00:13:21.839 --> 00:13:25.029
Why somebody whose name out there could be
a problem.

00:13:25.029 --> 00:13:30.670
But for others, it's a it's a it's a kind
of a circuitous route around citing your sources.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:34.420
So part of our work in the media, Amy, and
part of our work in critical media literacy

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.420
is evidence based research.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.760
We want to provide evidence ourselves, and
we want people to be looking for evidence

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:41.760
themselves.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:46.140
Speaker 1: There was even confusion during
the Trump era of when we talk about an anonymous

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:49.010
source, are they known to the reporter?

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:54.449
And there were countless instances where people
misunderstood that even the reporter didn't

00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:57.180
know who the person was when the reporter
did.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.690
It's just they weren't they weren't naming
them.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:00.070
And that's a critical difference.

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.550
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:02.550
Absolutely. 100%.

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:07.709
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with Allison
Butler, contributor to the Media and Media

00:14:07.709 --> 00:14:12.030
Guide to Critical Media Literacy for Young
People, also director of the Media Literacy

